{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And Canaan begot Sidon his firstborn</b> — These are the ten nations, sons of Canaan, who were given to Abraham our father (Genesis 15:19–21), for all the seed of Canaan was sold as an everlasting servant, and these are the ones who were given to him. But the name changed for most of them in the days of Abraham, for here they are written by the name their father called them on the day they were born, and after they separated into their lands, to their nations, they were called by other names. Perhaps they took for themselves a name based on the name of the land, as we explained (Ramban on Genesis 10:13), and likewise \"Seir the Horite\" (Genesis 36:20) — Seir is the name of the city — and so many others. Or perhaps the Arkite and the Sinite begot families that were cut off from them, and their descendants became the Kenite and the Kenizzite by way of illustration, and they became heads of ancestral houses; the nation would be called by their name, as is the custom with the tribes of Israel. And so He called them by the name they had in the days of Abraham in His gift to him.\n\nAnd a proof for this matter: the Hivite mentioned here is not mentioned in the gift to Abraham, yet he was among them, as it is stated (Deuteronomy 7:1): \"and He will dislodge many nations from before you — the Hittite and the Girgashite and the Amorite and the Canaanite and the Perizzite and the Hivite and the Jebusite, seven nations\" — and so in every place. And He counts the Canaanite together with his sons and counts among them only ten, for one of his sons did not prevail like his brothers and was called together with his brothers by his father's name. And it is possible that Sidon his firstborn was the one called \"Canaanite\" together with one of his brothers who did not become a nation.\n\nAnd do not find it difficult concerning the land of the Philistines, which was given to Abraham, as it is written (Genesis 26:3): \"Sojourn in this land, and I will give to you and to your seed all these lands\" — and they are descendants of Mitzrayim — for Scripture says (Joshua 13:3): \"To the Canaanite shall be reckoned the five lords of the Philistines,\" for they conquered part of the land and dwelt in it. And here you will see that the border of the Canaanite was from Sidon, coming to Gerar, as far as Gaza, and these are cities of the Philistines, for Abimelech king of Gerar — and Gaza is for the Azzaite — and likewise \"for Gaza one\" (I Samuel 6:17), and Sidon belongs to the Philistines, as it is written (Joshua 13:2–6): \"All the Sidonians I will dispossess from before the children of Israel; only allot it as an inheritance,\" and it is written (Joel 4:4): \"And moreover, what are you to Me, Tyre and Sidon, and all the regions of Philistia?\" Perhaps the rest of the land of the Philistines did not belong to Israel but only their five lords.\n\nAnd know that the land of Canaan, according to its borders, has from of old been fit for Israel and is the territory of their inheritance, as it is stated (Deuteronomy 32:8): \"When the Most High gave the nations their inheritance, when He separated the children of man, He set the borders of the peoples according to the number of the children of Israel.\" But the Holy One, blessed be He, gave it to Canaan at the time of the Dispersion because he was a servant — to guard it for Israel — like a man who deposits the master's son's property with his servant until he grows up and becomes entitled to the property and also to the servant. And I will explain further, with the help of El the Most High (Deuteronomy 2:23)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The citation from Joel 4:4 follows the verse numbering in the Hebrew Bible (Tanakh); this corresponds to Joel 3:4 in some English Bible numbering conventions."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:27:18.267330523Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}